# _headers — Netlify / Cloudflare Pages HTTP header rules
#
# GitHub Pages ignores this file entirely (no custom-headers support),
# which is why the CSP has lived only in the <meta http-equiv> tag in
# index.html so far. Netlify and Cloudflare Pages both read a file
# literally named `_headers` at the root of the published output and
# apply real HTTP response headers from it — same syntax on both, no
# config beyond dropping this file next to index.html.
#
# The <meta> CSP tag in index.html is left in place on purpose: it still
# works as a fallback on any host (including GitHub Pages) that doesn't
# read this file, and having both is harmless — browsers enforce the
# combination. The header below is the same policy, plus a couple of
# directives (frame-ancestors) that only take effect as a real header —
# CSP delivered via <meta> silently ignores those.
#
# Bump this file's rules any time index.html's CSP <meta> tag changes —
# they should stay in sync the same way APP_VERSION/CACHE_NAME do (see
# README.md).

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; worker-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), microphone=(), camera=(), payment=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains

# --- Cache control -----------------------------------------------------
# None of this app's mutable files are content-hashed in their filename
# (app.js, lib/pdf.min.mjs, etc. keep the same name every deploy), and
# sw.js's fetch handler does its own stale-while-revalidate over the
# network on top of whatever the HTTP layer hands it. If a CDN/browser
# caches these aggressively, an updated CACHE_NAME bump can still end up
# serving old file contents until that HTTP-level cache expires — so the
# shell files below are set to always revalidate. Only the PNG icons
# (genuinely static day-to-day) get a real cache lifetime.

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/app.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/manifest.json
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/lib/*
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# Listed individually rather than a /*.png glob — Netlify/Cloudflare
# Pages wildcard matching on a bare suffix pattern like /*.png has been
# reported to over-match other file types, which would be worse than no
# rule at all here.
/icon-192.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
/icon-512.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
/icon-maskable-512.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
/apple-touch-icon.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
